  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "major-scales-applied",
      "title": "Major Scales — Applied",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** theory | **Tier:** applied | **Difficulty:** 3/10 | **Time:** ~30 min\n**Prerequisites:** Major Scales — Introduction\n**Tags:** scales, major, keys, circle-of-fifths"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Applied tier: construct any major scale, read key signatures, and relate scales to practical keys on your instrument. Revisit WWHWWWH from the intro tier — here you use it for transposition and sight-reading."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-signatures-and-the-circle-of-fifths",
      "title": "Key signatures and the circle of fifths",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Each sharp key adds one sharp in order: **F♯, C♯, G♯, D♯, A♯, E♯, B♯**.\n\nEach flat key adds one flat: **B♭, E♭, A♭, D♭, G♭, C♭, F♭**.\n\nMoving **clockwise** on the circle of fifths raises the key by a fifth (adds a sharp). Counter-clockwise adds a flat."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tetrachords",
      "title": "Tetrachords",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "A major scale splits into two **tetrachords** (W-W-H each). Upper tetrachord of C (G-A-B-C) mirrors the lower (C-D-E-F). Splitting scales this way speeds mental transposition: learn the tetrachord shapes, then join them at the tonic."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "practical-keys",
      "title": "Practical keys",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Fiddle, whistle, and guitar session repertoire clusters in **G, D, A, C, E** major (and related modes). Drill scales in these keys until fingerings are automatic before adding remote signatures."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spiral-link",
      "title": "Spiral link",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Intervals (next unit) measure the distances you have been counting as whole and half steps. Cadences (later) explain why degree 5 and degree 7 feel like \"going home.\""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "entities": [],
  "entity_order": [],
  "playlist": [],
  "tunes": [],
