  "reading_list": [],
  "quiz_markdown": "### Q1. Order of sharps begins:\n\n- A) F♯ C♯ G♯\n- B) B♭ E♭ A♭\n- C) F♯ B♭ E♭\n- D) C♯ G♯ D♯ only\n\n**Answer:** A\n\n### Q2. A tetrachord in major follows W-W-H.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q3. D major has two sharps: F♯ and C♯.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q4. Moving clockwise on the circle of fifths generally adds a sharp.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q5. A major has three sharps.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q6. F major has one flat: B♭.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q7. Two identical tetrachords stack to form a major scale.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q8. E major is a common session key on fiddle.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q9. Key signature tells which accidentals apply throughout a piece.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q10. Transposing a melody from G to D raises every note a perfect fifth.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q11. B♭ major has two flats.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q12. Applied scale work supports reading lead sheets in multiple keys.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q13. Lower tetrachord of G major is G-A-B-C.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q14. Upper tetrachord of G major is D-E-F♯-G.\n\n**Answer:** True",
